/-!
# The exact sequence of a sum of effective Cartier divisors

Let `X` be a scheme, let `D ⊆ D'` be effective Cartier divisors on `X`, and let `C` be an
effective Cartier divisor with `D' = D + C`.  Then the structure sheaf of `D'` is an extension
of the structure sheaf of `D` by the restriction `𝒪_X(-D)|_C` of the ideal sheaf of `D` to `C`:

`0 ⟶ 𝒪_X(-D)|_C ⟶ 𝒪_{D'} ⟶ 𝒪_D ⟶ 0`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Divisors.addEffectiveCartierDivisor_shortExact`: **Stacks Tag 0C4T.**

## Implementation notes

The sum of two effective Cartier divisors is the closed subscheme cut out by the *product* of
their ideal sheaves, which is Mathlib's `Mul` on `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData`; the
hypothesis is therefore `D' = D * C` and not `D' = D + C`, the latter being the
scheme-theoretic intersection.  Correspondingly the inclusion `D ⊆ D'` of closed subschemes is
`D' ≤ D` on ideal sheaf data.  It follows from `D' = D * C`, but is taken as a hypothesis
because the restriction morphism `𝒪_{D'} ⟶ 𝒪_D` is indexed by a proof of it.

Of the three effective Cartier hypotheses of the source only the one on `D` is needed, and only
it is taken: the sequence exists for any ideal sheaf `C` with `D' = D * C`, since the left-hand
term is the tensor product of the ideal sequence of `C` with the invertible sheaf `ℐ_D`, and
only `ℐ_D` is required to be invertible.

The proof follows the source in two steps.  Writing `i : C ⟶ X` for the inclusion:

* the two ideal sequences of Tag 0C6K, for `D'` and for `D`, have the same middle term `𝒪_X`,
  and the restriction `𝒪_{D'} ⟶ 𝒪_D` is compatible with their quotient maps
  (`quotientRestriction_diagram`); `shortExact_kernels_of_quotients` turns that into a short
  exact sequence whose left-hand term is the kernel of `ℐ_D ⟶ ℐ_D/ℐ_{D'}`;
* that kernel is `i_* i^* ℐ_D`.  The ideal sequence of `C` tensored by the invertible sheaf
  `ℐ_D` is short exact (`shortExact_unitAtStructureSheaf_tensor`), its right-hand term is
  `i_* i^* ℐ_D` by the projection formula of Tag 0FZE for a closed immersion together with the
  pullback-tensor comparison of Tag 01CD (`projectionIso`), and its left-hand term is
  `ℐ_{D'} = ℐ_C · ℐ_D` because multiplication of sections is an isomorphism
  `ℐ_C ⊗ ℐ_D ≅ ℐ_{C · D}` (`idealTensorMulIso`).

`idealTensorMulIso` is the only new mathematics.  It is an epimorphism because over an affine
open the ideal of the product is generated by products of sections of the two factors
(`Stacks.Divisors.mem_ideal_iff_exists_idealSheafSection` and `Submodule.mul_induction_on`), and
a monomorphism because tensoring by an invertible sheaf of modules is an equivalence; Tag 01QY,
which identifies quasi-coherent modules on a closed subscheme with ones on `X`, is not needed in
this form, because the projection formula for a closed immersion carries no quasi-coherence
hypothesis.

**Two readings of the category of `𝒪_X`-modules are in use and the comparison is paid exactly
once.** The ideal sheaves, the tensor product and the projection formula are stated over
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf X.sheaf`, while the structure sheaf of a closed
subscheme and the restriction between two of them are stated over
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules`.  The two are the same category, by the definitional
identifications `Stacks.SchemeCohomology.ringSheaf_ringedSpace`,
`Stacks.SchemeCohomology.modulePushforward_hom` and `AlgebraicGeometry.pullback_scheme_eq`.  A
`CategoryTheory.ShortComplex`, however, carries its `Preadditive` instance as a parameter, and
the two readings supply different ones: `SheafOfModules.instPreadditive` for the unwrapped
category and the `Preadditive` of the `Abelian` structure for the wrapper, which is the one the
diagram lemma uses.  `toSchemeComplex` and `shortExact_toSchemeComplex` discharge that
comparison in a single declaration each; without them it is re-elaborated inside every
statement mentioning a short complex.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0C4T](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0C4T)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02ON](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02ON)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01QY](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01QY)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0C6K](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0C6K)
-/

